  of poured out of me i just it just kind  of fell into place in my head  so so what flowed out of you because  number one we should say the  illustrations in this comic book we've  all read it are spectacular yes  they're really they they they just  conjure up such emotions so  when jules looks in the binoculars what  does he see what was your conceit here  well the the conceit is is that he gets  a pair of binoculars  uh inherited um from his grandfather who  was a  civil rights activist and korean war vet  and he thinks our  crappy old pair of binoculars and  doesn't want to deal with it  but when he starts looking through them  he sees not only the birds but he starts  seeing  these um african-americans who have been  unjustly killed at the hands of police  and he doesn't understand what he's  seeing at first and it's in the course  of the story how  he goes from being this flipping  teenager to a very sober young man who  who starts to appreciate what's been  going on  and so that that's the conceit of the  story but the idea is to weave together  all these different experiences you know  experiences i've had experiences  that others have had the experiences  we've all seen over the summer and  before  of african americans who have lost their  lives and by  transcending the specifics of any of  those individual moments  getting to the heart of the matter you  know what's going on here which is the  systemic bias  that that pervades all these incidents  christian you've made a  there's a quote that i read of yours  which i find very powerful which is that  people have to stop othering each other  what do you mean  well we have to recognize when we're  looking at something else that we're  looking at somebody's  uh somebody's brother somebody's sister  somebody's father somebody's mother you  know somebody's uncle cousin whatever  you know that  we're not looking at a thug we're not  you know we shouldn't presume that  someone is a criminal  um and i think that's very important i  think that's that's  what's what has the potential to save us  as a  society at this moment when we're so  divided  yeah christian uh tony decouple here you  don't have to convince me about the  power of a comic book and why it's a  great vehicle for a story like this but  for people who might be surprised that  it's in comic book form  i'd love to hear you talk about why a  comic book works  oh we lost him for a moment i think well  he dropped his ifb  yeah so it says to put it back in his  ear the earpiece just dropped out  all right and we saw yeah you can hear  us now christian okay here we go so i  was asking about the power of a comic  book in particular that form to tell a  story like this could you talk about  why you like it as a genre sure um  the power of comics and mark morale is  the anchor on this story  and i want to give a shout out to lethal  martinez  but mark morales said it best he said  you know comic books combine  both the pictures the images and  words so you get the power of both in  one place  and i think that visual storytelling and  sequential story  storytelling um with words and pictures  just has a power that no other medium  really has  yeah in it jules has an encounter with a  woman named beth cooper in central park  with her dog and you know jewelers her  name's not  okay okay you're right you're right  sorry sorry sorry sorry chris sorry  he has an encounter with a woman named  beth and you want to be very clear that  it's not amy cooper but  who does she represent i know my bad  what does she represent  well i mean you know obviously it's  inspired by you know  that experience but also other  experiences um she represents  she doesn't represent any one person or  any one thing  um you know that's a pastiche of a lot  of encounters i've had  and encounters that other people have  had um so  i don't know that she she represents a  she doesn't represent a particular  person um but  she represents sort of someone who's  mired  in that bias and doesn't recognize it  have you and amy cooper spoken since  that day  spoken or talked no no  any desire to do so um  i i don't see the point i'm i'm not  looking for a jerry springer moment  where you know or a hair pulling fight  or you know  a feel-good hug it out moment which has  no you know substance or meaning behind  it  so you know what i'm really about is is  sort of moving the conversation forward  yeah and if if there was a way that the  two of us getting together would do that  sure i'd be all for that but you'd have  a hard time convincing me that it  wouldn't turn into you know a sideshow a  spectacle just for  you know people to pat themselves on the  back that's that doesn't serve anybody  i think it's really focused on go ahead  go ahead  well i was just going to say what i'm  really focused on is you know  let's not get distracted there are a lot  of people who have a vested interest  right now in distracting us  from tackling the systemic bias you know  whether it's a couple of wild  individuals who want to throw bricks  through windows  or a couple of right-wing individuals  want to dress up like left-wing  individuals and throw bricks through  windows  disturb trouble that they can then use  their guns to shoot at  you know or whether it's some charlatan  in a high place who wants to  distract us from his many many failures  a lot of people have a vested interest  in distracting us right now  one thing and the point is don't be  distracted focus on this issue  just as jules does when he turns away  from that person who's trying to make it  all about her  and he focuses on those people and  that's the point focus on those we have  lost and how we keep from losing any  more  it's so well said but the thing that  struck me about the comic book chris is  that jules wears glasses  why was that important to you do your  demonstration what you said about  glasses i  i never thought of it until you pointed  it out one day  